examples.question_text:
A committee of $4$ is chosen from $9$ people, including Ava and Ben. How many committees are possible if:
(a) Ava must be included;
(b) Ben must be excluded;
(c) Ava must be included and Ben must be excluded?

Eight points are marked in a plane. Exactly four, $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$, lie on one straight line, and no other three points are collinear. How many triangles can be formed using the points as vertices?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(5,0);
\fill (0.4,0) circle (2pt);
\fill (1.7,0) circle (2pt);
\fill (3.1,0) circle (2pt);
\fill (4.6,0) circle (2pt);
\fill (0.6,1.4) circle (2pt);
\fill (1.4,3.2) circle (2pt);
\fill (3.3,2.7) circle (2pt);
\fill (4.7,1.9) circle (2pt);
\path (0.4,0) node[below] {$A$};
\path (1.7,0) node[below] {$B$};
\path (3.1,0) node[below] {$C$};
\path (4.6,0) node[below] {$D$};
\path (0.6,1.4) node[left] {$E$};
\path (1.4,3.2) node[above] {$F$};
\path (3.3,2.7) node[above] {$G$};
\path (4.7,1.9) node[right] {$H$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Nine distinct points lie on a circle. How many unordered pairs of triangles can be formed using these points as vertices if the triangles have no vertices in common? The triangles may overlap.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) circle (2cm);
\foreach \i in {1,...,9}{
\pgfmathsetmacro{\a}{90-40*(\i-1)}
\fill (\a:2) circle (2pt);
\path (\a:2.35) node {$P_{\i}$};
}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
